# Development & Git Workflow

## npm Scripts

```bash
# Development
npm run dev              # Start development server
npm run dev:turbo        # Start with Turbopack (faster)

# Building and Production
npm run build            # Build for production
npm run start            # Start production server

# Code Quality
npm run lint             # Run ESLint
npm run format           # Format code with Prettier
npm run format:check     # Check formatting
npm run type-check       # Run TypeScript type checking
npm run check-all        # Run all quality checks
npm run ci               # CI pipeline (lint + format + type-check + build)

# Maintenance
npm run clean            # Clean build artifacts
```

## Code Quality

### Pre-commit Checks

Always run before committing:

```bash
npm run check-all
```

### Tools

- **ESLint**: Next.js core web vitals + TypeScript strict mode
- **Prettier**: Automatic formatting on save
- **TypeScript**: Strict mode enabled

## Git Branch Naming

### Format

```
<prefix>/<descriptive-name>
```

### Prefixes

| Prefix   | Use Case                      | Example                         |
| :------- | :---------------------------- | :------------------------------ |
| feature  | New features or functionality | `feature-user-authentication`   |
| fix      | Bug fixes and corrections     | `fix-navigation-overflow`       |
| refactor | Code refactoring              | `refactor-service-architecture` |
| docs     | Documentation updates         | `docs-update-api-reference`     |
| perf     | Performance improvements      | `perf-optimize-image-loading`   |
| test     | Adding or updating tests      | `test-add-component-tests`      |
| chore    | Maintenance tasks             | `chore-update-dependencies`     |

### Naming Guidelines

✅ **DO:**

- Use lowercase letters
- Use hyphens to separate words
- Be descriptive but concise
- Use imperative mood ("add" not "adding")

❌ **DON'T:**

- Use spaces or special characters
- Use vague names like `fix-bug`
- Include issue numbers in branch name

## Commit Message Format

### Structure

```
<type>[optional scope]: <emoji> <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

### Type and Emoji Reference

| Type     | Emoji | Description                  |
| :------- | :---- | :--------------------------- |
| feat     | ✨    | New feature or functionality |
| fix      | 🐛    | Bug fix                      |
| docs     | 📚    | Documentation changes only   |
| style    | 💎    | Code style changes           |
| refactor | 🔨    | Code refactoring             |
| perf     | 🚀    | Performance improvements     |
| test     | 🚨    | Adding or updating tests     |
| build    | 📦    | Build system changes         |
| ci       | 👷    | CI/CD configuration changes  |
| chore    | 🔧    | Other maintenance tasks      |
| revert   | ⏪    | Revert a previous commit     |

### Examples

```bash
# Feature
git commit -m "feat: ✨ add dark mode toggle"

# Bug fix
git commit -m "fix: 🐛 resolve navigation menu overflow"

# Documentation
git commit -m "docs: 📚 update installation instructions"

# With scope
git commit -m "feat(api): ✨ add contact form validation endpoint"

# With body and footer
git commit -m "fix: 🐛 resolve mobile menu overflow

The mobile menu was overflowing on small screens.
Updated responsive breakpoints and added scroll behavior.

Fixes #78"
```

### Breaking Changes

Include an exclamation mark and `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer:

```bash
git commit -m "feat!: ✨ migrate to TypeScript 5.0

BREAKING CHANGE: requires Node.js 18+ for TypeScript 5.0 support"
```

## Workflow Steps

### 1. Create Branch

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b feature/my-awesome-feature
```

### 2. Make Changes

- Write clean, maintainable code
- Follow project conventions
- Add tests if applicable
- Update documentation if needed

### 3. Commit Changes

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "feat: ✨ add awesome feature"
```

### 4. Keep Updated

```bash
# Rebase on main
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main

# Or merge if you prefer
git merge origin/main
```

### 5. Push

```bash
git push origin feature/my-awesome-feature
```

### 6. Open Pull Request

**PR Requirements:**

- Clear title following conventional commit format
- Summary of changes
- Screenshots for UI changes
- Links to related issues
- All CI checks pass

## Common Scenarios

### Working on Multiple Features

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b feature/second-feature
```

### Fixing Production Bugs

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout -b fix/critical-security-issue
# After fixing
git push origin fix/critical-security-issue
# Open PR to main
```

### Resolving Conflicts

```bash
# During rebase/merge
# 1. Fix conflicts in your editor
# 2. Stage resolved files
git add .
# 3. Continue rebase
git rebase --continue
```

## Best Practices

### Commit Frequency

- Commit often with meaningful messages
- Each commit represents a logical unit of work
- Don't commit broken code to shared branches

### Branch Lifespan

- Keep branches short-lived (days, not weeks)
- Delete merged branches
- Merge or close stale branches

### Scopes (Optional)

Common scopes in this project:

- `api` - API routes and endpoints
- `ui` - UI components and styling
- `docs` - Documentation files
- `deps` - Dependencies updates
- `config` - Configuration files
- `ci` - CI/CD workflows
- `seo` - SEO-related changes
- `a11y` - Accessibility improvements

